One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when performing file operations, leading to **Path Traversal** attacks. An attacker can provide inputs like `../../etc/passwd` to access sensitive files outside the intended directory.

```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class FileServer {
    // This method is vulnerable because it concatenates user input directly into a file path
    public void readFile(String userFilename) throws IOException {
        File file = new File("C:/app/user_uploads/" + userFilename);

        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(file);
        while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
            System.out.println(scanner.nextLine());
        }
        scanner.close();
    }
}
***

import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileServer {
    // This method is secure as it normalizes the path and verifies the file stays within the intended directory
    public void readFile(String userFilename) throws IOException {
        Path baseDirectory = Paths.get("C:/app/user_uploads/").toAbsolutePath().normalize();
        // Normalizes the input to prevent ../../ sequences
        Path resolvedPath = baseDirectory.resolve(userFilename).normalize();

        // Security Check: Ensure the resulting path is still a child of the base directory
        if (!resolvedPath.startsWith(baseDirectory)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Invalid file path attempt!");
        }

        File file = resolvedPath.toFile();
        if (file.exists() && file.isFile()) {
            java.util.Scanner scanner = new java.util.Scanner(file);
            while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
                System.out.println(scanner.nextLine());
            }
            scanner.close();
        }
    }
}
```

